{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Power Set.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Power Set.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"power set power set","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Power Set.md","fileName":"Power Set.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Power Set","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-16 22:54\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Set Theory and Relations]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Set Theory and Relations","Subset","Uncountable Set","FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Power Set.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Power Set.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"power set power set","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Power Set.md","fileName":"Power Set.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Power Set","heading":"Power Set","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"The power set P(A) is the set of every [[Subset]] of A. It always contains the empty set and A itself; if A is finite with n elements, then P(A) has 2^n elements.\n\nPower sets distinguish membership from containment: ∅ is a subset of every set and therefore an element of every power set. Cantor's result that no set is bijective with its power set also drives the distinction between countable and [[Uncountable Set|uncountable sets]].","tags":["baby"],"links":["Set Theory and Relations","Subset","Uncountable Set","FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Power Set.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Power Set.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"power set power set","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Power Set.md","fileName":"Power Set.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Power Set","heading":"References","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"[[FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Set Theory and Relations","Subset","Uncountable Set","FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""}]}